    "text": "The necessity of explicit translation of long-term memory into plans depends on the temporal horizon and complexity of the memory dependencies required to resolve partial observability and state overwrites, which may differ between the tasks in reactive policy benchmarks and those targeted by the WorldLines framework.",
    "topic": "task dependency"
  },
  {
    "text": "Reactive policies with implicit state representations (e.g., RNNs) can handle partial observability and overwritten states within a bounded effective memory horizon, but may degrade when the horizon exceeds the model's capacity, whereas explicit memory-to-plan translation aims to extend that horizon systematically.",
    "topic": "memory horizon bounds"
  },
  {
    "text": "Partial observability and overwritten world states are necessary preconditions for requiring explicit long-term memory translation only for tasks where the relevant past information cannot be losslessly compressed into a fixed-size hidden state, which is an empirical claim testable by comparing policy architectures on identical tasks with progressively longer memory demands.",
    "topic": "sufficiency condition"
  },
  {
    "text": "A direct empirical comparison on shared benchmarks, where the same long-horizon stateful tasks are solved by both reactive implicit-memory policies and explicit memory-to-plan systems, would determine whether explicit translation is necessary or if implicit representations suffice when scaled appropriately.",
    "topic": "empirical resolution"
  }
]
